---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SnapshotLab from "../../components/SnapshotLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D28.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("snapshots");
const DS = "module/zfs/dsl_dataset.c";
const DL = "module/zfs/dsl_deadlist.c";
const DD = "module/zfs/dsl_destroy.c";
const L = {
  alloc: gh("module/zfs/metaslab.c", 6061),
  free: gh(DS, 312, 315),
  elseBranch: gh(DS, 332),
  insert: gh(DS, 344),
  order: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 747, 753),
  crtxg: gh(DS, 1775),
  handOff: gh(DS, 1776),
  newDl: gh(DS, 1830, 1839),
  setPrev: gh(DS, 1881, 1884),
  eagain: gh(DS, 1534, 1539),
  zapKey: gh(DL, 544, 545),
  notInclusive: gh(DL, 521, 524),
  moveBpobj: gh(DL, 930, 952),
  freeBpobj: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 3828, 3835),
  insertBpobj: gh(DL, 829, 834),
  removeKey: gh(DL, 549, 577),
  headKey: gh(DD, 475, 479),
  numChildren: gh(DS, 1243, 1244),
  branch: gh(DD, 83, 87),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="snapshots"
  title="Why doesn’t deleting a file give me my space back? Snapshots and clones · Uberblok"
  description="A ZFS snapshot copies one block pointer, and one comparison of birth txgs decides whether a block the filesystem lets go of is freed or kept for a snapshot. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>Why doesn’t deleting a file give me my space back?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          A snapshot keeps every block that existed when it was taken. If the file was already there at your
          last snapshot, deleting it only takes it out of the live filesystem: its blocks stay on disk for the snapshot,
          and the space comes back when the last snapshot that holds them is destroyed. Taking a snapshot costs almost
          nothing, because ZFS never changes a block in place.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS writes in batches called transaction groups, or txgs, numbered in order, and every block records the txg
          it was written in, its birth txg. When the filesystem lets go of a block, ZFS compares that birth with the txg
          of its latest snapshot. A block born after it is in no snapshot and is freed. Any other block goes on the
          filesystem's deadlist, the list of blocks it no longer uses but a snapshot still holds.
        </p>
        <p>
          The deadlist is split by the txgs of the snapshots, so destroying one frees whole the parts only it held,
          without checking the blocks one by one. A clone, a writable filesystem made from a snapshot, shares every
          block with that snapshot at first and uses the same comparison for what it writes later. The snapshot cannot
          be destroyed while the clone exists.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Take a snapshot, change blocks, destroy it</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Select B in <code>fs</code>, overwrite it and sync. The line under the buttons says which old blocks were freed
          and which were kept for a snapshot, and a ring marks them on the board. Take a snapshot and overwrite B again to
          see the same comparison, this time with the new snapshot. Each snapshot says what destroying it would free: destroy
          one, sync, and those blocks go. Clone a snapshot and overwrite a block in the clone: the old block stays,
          because the snapshot still holds it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>fs</code> has room for four data blocks, A to D, under one root block. The small number on each block is
          its birth txg: A<sub>8</sub> was born in txg 8. Select any block to see which snapshots hold it. Under the
          figure, each sync is listed step by step, with the two numbers it compared.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SnapshotLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figure follows five places in the code of datasets and deadlists. Open a Source note to see the lines,
        taken from release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Free it, or keep it for a snapshot</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Every block a filesystem lets go of goes through <code>dsl_dataset_block_kill</code>, in the txg being
          written. One test makes the whole decision: the block's birth against <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code>, the
          txg of the filesystem's latest snapshot.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>dsl_dataset_block_kill</code>, dsl_dataset.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={DS}
            from={294}
            to={294}
            symbol="dsl_dataset_block_kill"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 294,
                text: `Born after the latest snapshot: <a href="${L.free}">freed</a> in this txg. Otherwise <a href="${L.elseBranch}">the <code>else</code></a> <a href="${L.insert}">puts it on the deadlist</a>. The birth was set <a href="${L.alloc}">when the block was allocated</a>. The test is strict: a block born in the txg of a snapshot is in that snapshot, because <a href="${L.order}">a txg writes its data before it takes its snapshots</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A snapshot copies one block pointer</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>dsl_dataset_snapshot_sync_impl</code> creates the snapshot at the end of a txg. It writes one new record
          for it, and the only thing it takes from the tree of blocks is the pointer to its root.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>dsl_dataset_snapshot_sync_impl</code>, dsl_dataset.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={DS}
            from={1782}
            to={1784}
            symbol="dsl_dataset_snapshot_sync_impl"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1783,
                text: `The snapshot's <code>ds_bp</code>, the pointer to the root of the tree, is the filesystem's. Nothing below it is read or written. The snapshot also gets <a href="${L.crtxg}">this txg as its creation txg</a> and <a href="${L.handOff}">the filesystem's deadlist as it is</a>. The filesystem starts <a href="${L.newDl}">a new, empty deadlist with one more key</a> and <a href="${L.setPrev}">records this txg as its latest snapshot</a>. A second snapshot in the same txg <a href="${L.eagain}">is refused</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Where a kept block goes</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A deadlist is split into entries, each keyed by the txg of a snapshot. <code>dsl_deadlist_insert</code> puts a
          block in the entry with the largest key below its birth, so the entry says between which two snapshots the
          block was born.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>dsl_deadlist_insert</code>, dsl_deadlist.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={DL}
            from={487}
            to={492}
            symbol="dsl_deadlist_insert"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 487,
                to: 488,
                text: `The block's birth is looked up among the keys, which are <a href="${L.zapKey}">stored on disk</a>. A key <a href="${L.notInclusive}">“is not inclusive”</a>: the entry &gt;&#8239;9 holds blocks born after txg 9.`,
              },
              { from: 489, to: 492, text: "With no exact match, it takes the nearest key below. With an exact match, it takes the key before that one. Either way, it is the largest key strictly below the birth." },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Destroying a snapshot</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>dsl_destroy_snapshot_sync_impl</code> uses those keys. The blocks that only the destroyed snapshot held
          were born after the snapshot before it and let go of before the next snapshot was taken, so they fill whole entries of the
          next dataset's deadlist.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>dsl_destroy_snapshot_sync_impl</code>, dsl_destroy.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={DD}
            from={415}
            to={424}
            symbol="dsl_destroy_snapshot_sync_impl"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 415,
                to: 418,
                text: `Every entry of the next deadlist <a href="${L.moveBpobj}">at or above</a> the snapshot's <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code> moves to the pool's free list, which <a href="${L.freeBpobj}">is freed in later txgs</a>. No block pointer is read to decide it. The figure frees those blocks at once.`,
              },
              { from: 419, to: 420, text: "Space accounting, not drawn." },
              {
                from: 422,
                to: 424,
                text: `The snapshot's own deadlist, blocks the snapshot before it still holds, <a href="${L.insertBpobj}">joins the next one, entry by entry</a>. Then its creation txg <a href="${L.removeKey}">is removed as a key</a> from the filesystem's deadlist, <a href="${L.headKey}">when the next dataset is a snapshot</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A clone starts from a snapshot</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>dsl_dataset_create_sync_dd</code>, given an origin snapshot, makes a clone the way a snapshot is made: by
          copying one block pointer. It also gives the clone the origin as its latest snapshot, so the same test as above
          keeps what the clone shares and frees what it wrote.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>dsl_dataset_create_sync_dd</code>, dsl_dataset.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={DS}
            from={1213}
            to={1227}
            symbol="dsl_dataset_create_sync_dd"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1216,
                to: 1218,
                text: "The clone's latest snapshot is its origin, with the origin's creation txg. A block born at or before it is shared with the origin and goes on the clone's deadlist; a block the clone wrote is freed.",
              },
              {
                from: 1225,
                to: 1227,
                text: `Its <code>ds_bp</code> is the origin's. The origin <a href="${L.numChildren}">gains a child</a>. With the dataset after it, that makes two, and a snapshot with more than one child <a href="${L.branch}">cannot be destroyed</a>, <code>EEXIST</code>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the functions above over one filesystem with room for four data blocks and up to four snapshots, and one
        clone that cannot itself be snapshotted or destroyed. The root block stands for the objset, the dnodes and the
        indirect blocks, which are let go of by the same rule. Gang blocks, dedup blocks and blocks shared by block cloning, which ZFS frees later
        rather than at once, are freed at once here, and so are the blocks of a destroyed snapshot. Space accounting
        (the used and referenced sizes) is not drawn, nor is the clone's livelist, a list of the blocks it wrote that
        makes destroying a clone fast; neither changes whether a block is freed or kept. Holds, deferred destroy, pools
        older than keyed deadlists, bookmarks and <code>zfs promote</code>, which swaps a clone with the filesystem of
        its origin, are left out.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="snapshots" />
</Base>

